\ Exactly measured candidate years between signed-wide gate positions.
\ This adds a correct candidate representation at very large absolute
\ day coordinates and gate indices; native year discovery still uses
\ the original 32-bit candidate record and must be migrated later.
\
\ A year is (opening gate, closing gate]; its duration is whole DAYS,
\ not the number of gate spans. At least six positive gate gaps are
\ required, and the day length must lie between 252 and 5778.
\ Since each gap is 42..963 days, also enforce 42*G <= L <= 963*G.
\ These physical inequalities reject inconsistent synthetic endpoints.
\ Ordered candidates compare day length first, then earlier signed-wide
\ opening coordinate. Gate indices never act as a tie-breaker.
\
\ Native signed arithmetic is used only for small constant factors.
\ All positions, differences, span counts and lengths are exact wide.
\ This module has not been compiled or run under CAL.

A wide year candidate has
  a wide signed day called opening day,
  a wide signed day called closing day,
  a wide signed day called opening index,
  a wide signed day called closing index,
  a wide quantity called span count,
  a wide quantity called year length,
  a number called valid.

The first wide candidate is a wide year candidate.
The second wide candidate is a wide year candidate.

To measure a wide year candidate:
  Put 0 into the wide year candidate's valid.
  Put "0" into the wide year candidate's year length.
  Put "0" into the wide year candidate's span count.
  Put the wide year candidate's closing day into a wide day difference.
  Subtract the wide year candidate's opening day from the wide day difference.
  If the wide day difference's polarity is not 1, exit.
  Put the wide day difference's magnitude into the wide year candidate's year length.
  Put the wide year candidate's closing index into a wide index difference.
  Subtract the wide year candidate's opening index from the wide index difference.
  If the wide index difference's polarity is not 1, exit.
  Put the wide index difference's magnitude into the wide year candidate's span count.
  Verify a wide year length and span count.

To verify a wide year length and span count:
  Put "6" into a minimum wide spans.
  If the wide year candidate's span count is numerically smaller than the minimum wide spans, exit.
  Put "252" into a minimum wide year days.
  If the wide year candidate's year length is numerically smaller than the minimum wide year days, exit.
  Put "5778" into a maximum wide year days.
  If the maximum wide year days is numerically smaller than the wide year candidate's year length, exit.
  Put the wide year candidate's span count into a shortest legal wide days.
  Multiply the shortest legal wide days by 42.
  If the wide year candidate's year length is numerically smaller than the shortest legal wide days, exit.
  Put the wide year candidate's span count into a longest legal wide days.
  Multiply the longest legal wide days by 963.
  If the longest legal wide days is numerically smaller than the wide year candidate's year length, exit.
  Put 1 into the wide year candidate's valid.

To decide if a wide year candidate contains a wide signed day:
  If the wide year candidate's valid is not 1, say no.
  If the wide year candidate's closing day numerically precedes the wide signed day, say no.
  If the wide year candidate's opening day numerically precedes the wide signed day, say yes.
  Say no.

To decide if a wide year candidate comes before another wide year candidate:
  If the wide year candidate's year length is numerically smaller than the other wide year candidate's year length, say yes.
  If the other wide year candidate's year length is numerically smaller than the wide year candidate's year length, say no.
  If the wide year candidate's opening day numerically precedes the other wide year candidate's opening day, say yes.
  Say no.
